{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Whatever parts the hoof, etc., and chews the cud.</b> It may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Ḥullin 59a), that any animal that parts the hoof chews the cud, except for the pig; and that is what the verse says — <b>whatever parts</b> etc. <b>chews the cud</b> — meaning, it is known that it chews the cud, and afterward it excludes the pig, which has departed from the general rule. And perhaps from the fact that it did not exclude it from the general rule immediately adjacent to the declaration of the general rule, it hinted that even the pig is included in the general rule, according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Rabbenu Baḥya in the name of Tanḥuma): why is its name called חֲזִיר (pig)? Because it is destined to return (לַחְזֹר) to be permitted. And from now on, the general rule of ״כֹּל מַפְרֶסֶת״ etc. — it ״מַעֲלַת״ etc. — nothing has departed from its general rule, and concerning one of the times [to come], it says of the pig that it parts the hoof etc. — [the prohibition] will not apply."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:15.438674820Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}